  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1108 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nThe forecast period starts out with clouds moving into the region \nfrom the west and fog developing around the Holdrege, NE and \nPhillipsburg, KS area. Clouds are expected to decrease in some areas \novernight, which, when paired with the combination of radiational \ncooling and light winds, would be conducive for fog development. Fog \npotential was expanded a bit for tonight/tomorrow morning, \nparticularly for eastern Nebraska. Could get some areas of patchy \ndense fog, especially toward the western edges of the CWA. With the \ncontinued cloud cover and fog potential, temperatures were kept \nsimilar to the previous forecast, with maybe a couple of spots \nreaching the freezing mark tomorrow morning. \n\nThe pattern shifts Saturday as a closed low sits just to the east of \nthe Baja Peninsula, a ridge extends south to north from western \nCalifornia to the Wyoming Black Hills, and westerly flow sets up \nover our CWA, transitioning to northerly through the day. Expected \nhigh temperatures are currently forecasted to be in the upper 50s to \nlow 60s; however, cloud cover could play a factor in this. A delay \nin skies clearing out may result in some slightly lower temperatures \nthan are currently forecasted. \n\nSunday and Monday, northwest flow persists over the region as a mid-\nlevel ridge shifts east and the low over Mexico lifts east-northeast \ninto central and northern Texas. Sunday highs will be very similar \nto Saturday, reaching the upper 50s with a few mid-60s possible for \nareas along the Kansas/Nebraska/Iowa/Missouri borders. By Monday, \nhighs will warm into the upper 60s to low 70s for the I-80 corridor \nand areas south. Areas north of I-80 can expect low to mid-60s. \n\nA cold front drops across northern Nebraska Monday morning, slightly \ncooling temperatures into the 60s for the entire CWA by Tuesday \nafternoon.\n\nAfter tonight, lows each night are expected to range from the mid-\n30s to mid-40s each night. Dry conditions are expected through the \nforecast period.",
